(* Content-type: application/vnd.wolfram.mathematica *)
(* Ricci Flow and the Curvature?Laplacian Relations - Computational Classnotes - CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 *)

Notebook[{
Cell["Ricci Flow and the Curvature\:2013Laplacian Relations", "Title"],
Cell["The heat equation, curvature as a geometric Laplacian, the maximum principle, energy and entropy, and reaction against diffusion \:2014 Curvature from the Laplacian, notebook 14", "Subtitle"],
Cell["\:00a9 2026 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"Ricci flow is a heat flow of curvature. This notebook follows G. Khan, An Illustrated Introduction to the Ricci Flow (arXiv:2201.04923, 2022), pp. 14\:201333, and makes each step concrete on the flow the Laplacian Curvature Lab runs (https://lossofgenerality.github.io/laplacian/lab/", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["Laplacian", "Curvature"]]], "_Lab.html#ricci): a bumpy metric e^{2u}(dx\:00b2 + dy\:00b2) on the flat torus. Every derivation is worked by hand in a Text cell, then checked on the kernel; the page\:2019s live readings are set beside Wolfram\:2019s; every chart on the page is redrawn as a numbered figure; the flow can be played with an animator."}], "Text"],
Cell["Conventions: \:0394 is the Laplace\:2013Beltrami operator, negative semidefinite (\:0394\:2080 = \:2202\:00b2/\:2202x\:00b2 + \:2202\:00b2/\:2202y\:00b2 on the flat torus [0, 2\:03c0)\:00b2); Ric(X, X) for unit X is n \:2212 1 times the mean sectional curvature of the planes containing X; on a surface Ric = K g. Evaluate from the top: the first cell clears the session.", "Text"],
Cell["0. Toolkit and the page\:2019s readings", "Section"],
Cell["ClearAll[\"Global`*\"];\nkcol[k_?NumericQ, s_] := With[{w = Clip[k/s, {-1, 1}]}, Blend[{RGBColor[0.10, 0.27, 0.75], GrayLevel[0.97], RGBColor[0.96, 0.47, 0.02]}, (1 + Sign[w] Abs[w]^0.55)/2]];\nkLegend[s_, label_] := BarLegend[{kcol[#, s] &, {-s, s}}, LegendLabel -> label];\norange = RGBColor[0.96, 0.47, 0.02]; blue = RGBColor[0.10, 0.27, 0.75];\ncmpRow[label_, q_, p_, w_] := {label, q, p, w, Abs[p - w]};\ncmpGrid[rows_] := Grid[Prepend[Map[If[NumericQ[#] && ! IntegerQ[#], NumberForm[N[#], 7], #] &, rows, {2}],\n   Style[#, Bold] & /@ {\"\", \"quantity\", \"page (JavaScript)\", \"Wolfram\", \"|difference|\"}],\n  Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left, Background -> {None, {1 -> GrayLevel[0.93]}}, FrameStyle -> GrayLevel[0.75]];", "Input"],
Cell["page = <|\"ricci\" -> <|\"a\" -> 0.3, \"b\" -> 0.249, \"N\" -> 32, \"maxK0\" -> 4.63274553095, \"t\" -> 2.00336484118, \"maxK\" -> 0.0589607806405, \"totalK\" -> -2.27388738831*^-17, \"start\" -> <|\"t\" -> 0, \"area\" -> 44.4643488708, \"energy\" -> 5.36072886058, \"K2\" -> 38.1791657969, \"entropy\" -> 3.56080482591, \"umin\" -> -0.507209863349, \"umax\" -> 0.507209863349, \"kmin\" -> -4.63274553095, \"kmax\" -> 1.28912204446|>, \"end\" -> <|\"t\" -> 2.00336484118, \"area\" -> 44.4867486686, \"energy\" -> 0.0485751778654, \"K2\" -> 0.0434005817792, \"entropy\" -> 3.67329926729, \"umin\" -> 0.00753501592274, \"umax\" -> 0.122834499805, \"kmin\" -> -0.0432199320327, \"kmax\" -> 0.0589607806405|>|>, \"rd\" -> <|\"blowT\" -> 1.32496109544, \"blowMax\" -> 60.3575681887, \"heatEnd\" -> 0.607308263911|>|>;", "Input"],
Cell["1. What the flow is", "Section"],
Cell["A Riemannian metric g is an inner product on tangent vectors at each point; lengths, angles, areas and curvature come from it. The sectional curvature K(X, Y) of the plane of unit vectors X, Y measures how a small geodesic triangle with legs \:03b5X and \:03b5Y falls short of a flat one: its third side is L(\:03b5) = \:03b5\:2016X \:2212 Y\:2016(1 \:2212 (1/12)K(X, Y)(1 + \:27e8X, Y\:27e9)\:03b5\:00b2) + O(\:03b5\:2074). Ricci flow (Hamilton 1982) is \:2202g/\:2202t = \:22122 Ric(g): directions of positive Ricci curvature shrink, directions of negative Ricci curvature stretch.", "Text"],
Cell["Worked by hand: L(\:03b5) on the unit sphere. Two geodesics of length \:03b5 from one point at angle \:03b8 end at points whose distance L satisfies the spherical law of cosines, cos L = cos\:00b2\:03b5 + sin\:00b2\:03b5 cos \:03b8. With \:2016X \:2212 Y\:2016 = 2 sin(\:03b8/2) and \:27e8X, Y\:27e9 = cos \:03b8, expanding to order \:03b5\:00b3 gives L = 2\:03b5 sin(\:03b8/2)(1 \:2212 (1 + cos \:03b8)\:03b5\:00b2/12 + \:2026): the formula with K = 1. The cell checks the expansion.", "Text"],
Cell["Lsph = ArcCos[Cos[\:03b5]^2 + Sin[\:03b5]^2 Cos[\:03b8]];\nnormXY = Sqrt[2 - 2 Cos[\:03b8]];  (* \:2016X \:2212 Y\:2016 for unit vectors at angle \:03b8, = 2 sin(\:03b8/2) *)\nSimplify[Normal[Series[Lsph, {\:03b5, 0, 3}]] - \:03b5 normXY (1 - (1 + Cos[\:03b8]) \:03b5^2/12), 0 < \:03b8 < \:03c0 && \:03b5 > 0]", "Input"],
Cell["Expected 0.", "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"Worked by hand: the flow on a surface. Ric = K g, so \:2202g/\:2202t = \:22122K g. With g = e^{2u} g\:2080 and g\:2080 flat, \:2202g/\:2202t = 2", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "t"]]], " g, hence ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "t"]]], " = \:2212K. Liouville\:2019s equation K = \:2212e^{\:22122u}\:0394\:2080u turns this into ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "t"]]], " = e^{\:22122u}\:0394\:2080u: a heat equation for u whose conductivity e^{\:22122u} depends on u. Examples: the stereographic sphere u = log(2/(1 + r\:00b2)) has K = 1; the half-plane u = \:2212log y has K = \:22121; the round sphere g = (1 \:2212 2t)g\:2080 solves the flow and vanishes at t = \:00bd."}], "Text"],
Cell["Kconformal[w_] := Simplify[-Exp[-2 w] (D[w, {x, 2}] + D[w, {y, 2}])];\n{Kconformal[Log[2/(1 + x^2 + y^2)]], Kconformal[Log[1/y]],\n Simplify[D[(1 - 2 t) {{1, 0}, {0, Sin[\:03b8]^2}}, t] + 2 {{1, 0}, {0, Sin[\:03b8]^2}}]}", "Input"],
Cell["Expected {1, \:22121, zero matrix}: for g = (1 \:2212 2t)g\:2080, Ric = g\:2080 (Ricci curvature does not change under constant scaling), so \:2202g/\:2202t = \:22122g\:2080 = \:22122 Ric.", "Text"],
Cell["2. Why \:22122: Ricci curvature is minus a Laplacian of the metric", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"The Laplacian has three faces: the trace of the Hessian; the mean-value defect \:0394f(x) = lim (2n/\:03b5\:00b2)(mean of f on the sphere ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["S", "\:03b5"]]], "(x) \:2212 f(x)); and the rate at which the gradient flow \:03b3\:2032 = \:2212\:2207f changes small volumes. Curvature has the same three: sectional curvature sits at second order in L(\:03b5); the Riemann tensor plays the Hessian (in normal coordinates ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["g", "ij"]]], " = ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03b4", "ij"]]], " \:2212 (1/3)", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["R", "ikjl"]]], "x^k x^l + \:2026); Ricci curvature is its trace, and it measures how narrow geodesic cones close up (Ric > 0) or open out (Ric < 0). In harmonic coordinates ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["Ric", "ij"]]], " = \:2212\:00bd", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:0394g", "ij"]]], " + lower-order terms, so \:22122 Ric = \:0394g + \:2026: the flow is a heat equation for the metric. With +2 Ric it could only run backwards."}], "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"Worked by hand, in three cells. (a) Sphere mean in \:211d\:00b3: averaging the Taylor expansion of f over the unit sphere kills odd terms and gives \:27e8", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["x", "i"]]], " ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["x", "j"]]], "\:27e9 = ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03b4", "ij"]]], "/3, so the mean of f on ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["S", "\:03b5"]]], " is f + (\:03b5\:00b2/6)\:0394f + \:2026, i.e. \:0394f = lim (6/\:03b5\:00b2)(mean \:2212 f), the formula with 2n = 6. (b) The flow x\:2032 = \:2212\:2207f changes the volume of a small cube at the rate \:2212div \:2207f = \:2212\:0394f. (c) On a surface in conformal coordinates ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["g", "ij"]]], " = e^{2u}", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03b4", "ij"]]], ", the exact Ricci tensor is ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["Ric", "ij"]]], " = K ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["g", "ij"]]], " = \:2212\:0394\:2080u ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03b4", "ij"]]], ", while \:2212\:00bd\:0394\:2080", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["g", "ij"]]], " = \:2212(\:0394\:2080u + 2|\:2207\:2080u|\:00b2)e^{2u}", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03b4", "ij"]]], ": they agree in the linear term and differ only by terms quadratic in u."}], "Text"],
Cell["fT = a0 + a1 x + a2 y + a3 z + b11 x^2 + b22 y^2 + b33 z^2 + b12 x y + b13 x z + b23 y z;\nsphMean = Integrate[(fT /. {x -> \:03b5 Sin[\:03b8] Cos[\:03c6], y -> \:03b5 Sin[\:03b8] Sin[\:03c6], z -> \:03b5 Cos[\:03b8]}) Sin[\:03b8], {\:03b8, 0, \:03c0}, {\:03c6, 0, 2 \:03c0}]/(4 \:03c0);\n{Simplify[6/\:03b5^2 (sphMean - a0) - Laplacian[fT, {x, y, z}]],\n Simplify[Tr[D[-Grad[fT, {x, y, z}], {{x, y, z}}]] + Laplacian[fT, {x, y, z}]]}", "Input"],
Cell["Expected {0, 0}: the mean-value face and the volume-compression face (the trace of the Jacobian of the flow \:2212\:2207f is \:2212\:0394f).", "Text"],
Cell["ric = -(D[w[x, y], {x, 2}] + D[w[x, y], {y, 2}]);\nhalfLap = -1/2 (D[Exp[2 w[x, y]], {x, 2}] + D[Exp[2 w[x, y]], {y, 2}]);\n{Simplify[halfLap + (D[w[x, y], {x, 2}] + D[w[x, y], {y, 2}] + 2 (D[w[x, y], x]^2 + D[w[x, y], y]^2)) Exp[2 w[x, y]]],\n Normal[Series[(halfLap - ric) /. w -> Function[{x, y}, s v[x, y]], {s, 0, 1}]]}", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{"Expected: 0 (the identity for \:2212\:00bd\:0394\:2080", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["g", "ij"]]], "), then 0 to first order in the size s of u: \:2212\:00bd\:0394\:2080", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["g", "ij"]]], " and ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["Ric", "ij"]]], " have the same linear part."}], "Text"],
Cell["3. Hot spots cool: the maximum principle", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"Worked by hand. Where u is largest its Hessian is negative semidefinite, so \:0394\:2080u \:2264 0 and ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "t"]]], " = e^{\:22122u}\:0394\:2080u \:2264 0: max u falls; likewise min u rises, until u is constant and the metric flat. For the curvature, differentiate K = \:2212e^{\:22122u}\:0394\:2080u: ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "t"]]], " = 2", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "t"]]], " e^{\:22122u}\:0394\:2080u \:2212 e^{\:22122u}\:0394\:2080", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "t"]]], " = 2(\:2212K)(\:2212K) + e^{\:22122u}\:0394\:2080K, that is ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "t"]]], " = ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:0394", "g"]]], " K + 2K\:00b2 with ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:0394", "g"]]], " = e^{\:22122u}\:0394\:2080. At a minimum of K both terms are \:2265 0, so min K never decreases. The cell checks the curvature equation for an arbitrary u(x, y, t) solving the flow."}], "Text"],
Cell["Kof[f_] := -Exp[-2 f] (D[f, {x, 2}] + D[f, {y, 2}]);\nflowRule = Derivative[0, 0, 1][w][x, y, t] -> Exp[-2 w[x, y, t]] (D[w[x, y, t], {x, 2}] + D[w[x, y, t], {y, 2}]);\nlhs = D[Kof[w[x, y, t]], t] //. {flowRule, Derivative[a_, b_, 1][w][x, y, t] :> D[Exp[-2 w[x, y, t]] (D[w[x, y, t], {x, 2}] + D[w[x, y, t], {y, 2}]), {x, a}, {y, b}]};\nrhs = Exp[-2 w[x, y, t]] (D[Kof[w[x, y, t]], {x, 2}] + D[Kof[w[x, y, t]], {y, 2}]) + 2 Kof[w[x, y, t]]^2;\nSimplify[lhs - rhs]", "Input"],
Cell["Expected 0.", "Text"],
Cell["4. Energy and entropy", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"Worked by hand: the heat equation. For ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "t"]]], " = \:0394u on a closed space, E = \:222bu\:00b2 has E\:2032 = 2\:222bu\:0394u = \:22122\:222b|\:2207u|\:00b2 \:2264 0 (integration by parts, no boundary): heat spreads. For u > 0, S = \:2212\:222bu ln u has S\:2032 = \:2212\:222b(ln u + 1)\:0394u = \:222b|\:2207u|\:00b2/u \:2265 0, the Fisher information: a second law. Perelman\:2019s breakthrough (2002) was a monotone quantity of this kind for Ricci flow, closer to a Fisher information than to an entropy. The cell checks both on the circle for the exact positive solution u = 2 + e^{\:2212t} cos x + \:00bde^{\:22124t} sin 2x."}], "Text"],
Cell["uh = 2 + Exp[-t] Cos[x] + Exp[-4 t] Sin[2 x]/2;\nEh = Integrate[uh^2, {x, 0, 2 \:03c0}];\nSh[\:03c4_?NumericQ] := NIntegrate[-uh Log[uh] /. t -> \:03c4, {x, 0, 2 \:03c0}, WorkingPrecision -> 30, PrecisionGoal -> 20];\n{Simplify[D[uh, t] - D[uh, {x, 2}]], Simplify[D[Eh, t] + 2 Integrate[D[uh, x]^2, {x, 0, 2 \:03c0}]],\n With[{t0 = 3/10, d = 10^-6}, {(Sh[t0 + d] - Sh[t0 - d])/(2 d), NIntegrate[D[uh, x]^2/uh /. t -> t0, {x, 0, 2 \:03c0}, WorkingPrecision -> 30]}]}", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{"Expected 0, 0 and two equal numbers (S\:2032 and ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:222bu", "x\:00b2"]]], "/u at t = 0.3)."}], "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"Worked by hand: the flow\:2019s own energy and entropy. Area A = \:222ce^{2u}: A\:2032 = 2\:222c\:0394\:2080u = 0 on the torus (\:22122\:222cK dA = 0, Gauss\:2013Bonnet). Energy D = \:222c|\:2207\:2080u|\:00b2: D\:2032 = \:22122\:222c\:0394\:2080u ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "t"]]], " = \:22122\:222ce^{\:22122u}(\:0394\:2080u)\:00b2 = \:22122\:222cK\:00b2 dA \:2264 0. Entropy of the area density \:03c1 = e^{2u}/A, S = \:2212\:222c\:03c1 ln \:03c1: since ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:222c\:03c1", "t"]]], " = 0 and ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03c1", "t"]]], " = 2\:0394\:2080u/A, S\:2032 = \:2212(2/A)\:222c\:0394\:2080u (2u \:2212 ln A) = (4/A)\:222c|\:2207\:2080u|\:00b2 = 4D/A \:2265 0, with maximum ln(4\:03c0\:00b2) = 3.676 exactly for the flat metric."}], "Text"],
Cell["The page\:2019s run (32 \:00d7 32 periodic grid, initial u = 0.3 cos x + 0.249 sin 2y cos(x + y), five-point Laplacian, explicit steps dt = 0.2h\:00b2e^{2 min u} to t \:2265 2), with A, D, S, \:222cK\:00b2 dA, min K, max u and min u recorded at every step.", "Text"],
Cell["ricciRun[a_, b_, N_] := Module[{h = 2. \:03c0/N, U, lap, Kf, t = 0., rec = {}, frames = {}, next = 0., stats},\n   U = Table[a Cos[i h] + b Sin[2 j h] Cos[i h + j h], {i, 0, N - 1}, {j, 0, N - 1}];\n   lap[W_] := (RotateLeft[W, {1, 0}] + RotateRight[W, {1, 0}] + RotateLeft[W, {0, 1}] + RotateRight[W, {0, 1}] - 4 W)/h^2;\n   Kf[W_] := -Exp[-2 W] lap[W];\n   stats[W_] := Module[{K = Kf[W], A = Total[Exp[2 W], 2] h^2, \:03c1}, \:03c1 = Exp[2 W]/A;\n     <|\"t\" -> t, \"area\" -> A, \"energy\" -> Total[(RotateLeft[W, {1, 0}] - W)^2 + (RotateLeft[W, {0, 1}] - W)^2, 2], \"K2\" -> Total[K^2 Exp[2 W], 2] h^2,\n       \"entropy\" -> -Total[\:03c1 Log[\:03c1], 2] h^2, \"kmin\" -> Min[K], \"maxK\" -> Max[Abs[K]], \"umax\" -> Max[W], \"umin\" -> Min[W]|>];\n   AppendTo[rec, stats[U]]; AppendTo[frames, {0., Kf[U]}]; next = 0.05;\n   While[t < 2, With[{dt = 0.2 h^2 Exp[2 Min[U]]}, U = U + dt Exp[-2 U] lap[U]; t += dt]; AppendTo[rec, stats[U]];\n    If[t >= next, AppendTo[frames, {t, Kf[U]}]; next += 0.05]];\n   AppendTo[frames, {t, Kf[U]}]; <|\"rec\" -> rec, \"frames\" -> frames|>];\nrun = ricciRun[0.3, 0.3 0.83, 32]; rec = run[\"rec\"];\ncmpGrid[Join[{cmpRow[\"run\", \"steps\", 0, Length[rec] - 1] /. {a_, b_, 0, n_, _} :> {a, b, \"\", n, \"\"},\n    cmpRow[\"\", \"max |K| at the end\", page[\"ricci\", \"maxK\"], rec[[-1, \"maxK\"]]], cmpRow[\"\", \"t at the end\", page[\"ricci\", \"t\"], rec[[-1, \"t\"]]]},\n   Flatten[Table[{cmpRow[If[q == \"area\", which, \"\"], q, page[\"ricci\", which, q], rec[[If[which == \"start\", 1, -1], q]]]},\n     {which, {\"start\", \"end\"}}, {q, {\"area\", \"energy\", \"entropy\", \"kmin\", \"umax\", \"umin\"}}], 2]]]", "Input"],
Cell["rates = Table[With[{p = rec[[k - 1]], q = rec[[k]], dt = rec[[k, \"t\"]] - rec[[k - 1, \"t\"]]},\n    {q[\"t\"], (q[\"energy\"] - p[\"energy\"])/dt, -(q[\"K2\"] + p[\"K2\"]), (q[\"entropy\"] - p[\"entropy\"])/dt, 2 (q[\"energy\"] + p[\"energy\"])/q[\"area\"]}], {k, 2, Length[rec]}];\nlate = Select[rates, #[[1]] > 0.2 &];\n<|\"largest relative gap D\:2032 vs \:22122\:222cK\:00b2dA (t > 0.2)\" -> Max[Abs[(#[[2]] - #[[3]])/#[[3]]] & /@ late],\n  \"largest relative gap S\:2032 vs 4D/A (t > 0.2)\" -> Max[Abs[(#[[4]] - #[[5]])/#[[5]]] & /@ late],\n  \"area drift, relative\" -> (rec[[-1, \"area\"]] - rec[[1, \"area\"]])/rec[[1, \"area\"]],\n  \"max u never rises, min u and min K never fall\" -> And @@ (Differences[#[\"umax\"] & /@ rec] ~Join~ (-Differences[#[\"umin\"] & /@ rec]) ~Join~ (-Differences[#[\"kmin\"] & /@ rec]) // Thread[# <= 10^-12] &),\n  \"ln 4\:03c0\:00b2\" -> N[Log[4 \:03c0^2]]|>", "Input"],
Cell["What to see: the rates agree to within 0.8% (energy) and 0.4% (entropy) after t = 0.2 (finite differences in time on a 32 \:00d7 32 grid); the area drifts by 5 \:00d7 10\:207b\:2074 of itself because the time steps are explicit, while the exact flow keeps it fixed; the maximum principle holds at every one of the steps; the page\:2019s start and end readings agree with Wolfram\:2019s to round-off.", "Text"],
Cell["With[{D0 = rec[[1, \"energy\"]], S0 = rec[[1, \"entropy\"]], Smax = Log[4 \:03c0^2]},\n ListLinePlot[{{#[\"t\"], #[\"energy\"]/D0} & /@ rec, {#[\"t\"], (#[\"entropy\"] - S0)/(Smax - S0)} & /@ rec}, PlotStyle -> {orange, blue}, PlotRange -> {0, 1.02},\n  AxesLabel -> {\"t\", \"fraction\"}, PlotLegends -> {\"energy D/D(0)\", \"entropy: share of the way to ln 4\:03c0\:00b2\"}, ImageSize -> 420]]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Fig. 1. ", FontWeight -> "Bold", FontColor -> RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11]], "Energy falls to 1% of its start while the entropy of the area density climbs most of the way to its flat-metric maximum ln 4\:03c0\:00b2 (the page\:2019s chart \:201cEnergy falls, entropy rises\:201d)."}], "Text"],
Cell["kField[{t_, K_}, s_] := ArrayPlot[Reverse[Transpose[K]], ColorFunction -> (kcol[#, s] &), ColorFunctionScaling -> False, Frame -> False,\n   PlotLabel -> Row[{\"t = \", NumberForm[t, {3, 2}], \"   colours \:00b1\", NumberForm[s, 3]}], ImageSize -> 200];\nRow[{kField[run[\"frames\"][[1]], Max[Abs[run[\"frames\"][[1, 2]]]]], kField[SelectFirst[run[\"frames\"], #[[1]] >= 0.5 &], Max[Abs[SelectFirst[run[\"frames\"], #[[1]] >= 0.5 &][[2]]]]],\n   kField[run[\"frames\"][[-1]], Max[Abs[run[\"frames\"][[-1, 2]]]]]}, \"  \"]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Fig. 2. ", FontWeight -> "Bold", FontColor -> RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11]], "The curvature field at t = 0, 0.5 and 2, each coloured on its own \:00b1max|K| as the page now does, so the pattern stays visible as it shrinks: what survives is the slowest mode, a band in x."}], "Text"],
Cell["The animator: play the flow; the readout gives t, max|K|, min K, the energy and the entropy at each frame. Choose a fixed colour scale to watch it fade to flat instead.", "Text"],
Cell["With[{nF = Length[run[\"frames\"]], recT = #[\"t\"] & /@ rec},\n Manipulate[With[{fr = run[\"frames\"][[mF]]}, With[{q = rec[[First[Nearest[recT -> \"Index\", fr[[1]]]]]]},\n    Column[{Legended[kField[fr, If[mScale == \"auto\", Max[Abs[fr[[2]]]], 1.5]], kLegend[If[mScale == \"auto\", Max[Abs[fr[[2]]]], 1.5], \"K\"]],\n      Grid[{{\"t\", q[\"t\"]}, {\"max |K|\", q[\"maxK\"]}, {\"min K\", q[\"kmin\"]}, {\"energy D\", q[\"energy\"]}, {\"entropy S\", q[\"entropy\"]}, {\"area A\", q[\"area\"]}}, Alignment -> Left]}]]],\n  {{mF, 1, \"frame\"}, 1, nF, 1, ControlType -> Animator, AnimationRate -> 8}, {{mScale, \"auto\", \"colours\"}, {\"auto\" -> \"follow max |K|\", \"fixed\" -> \"fixed \:00b11.5\"}}, SaveDefinitions -> True]]", "Input"],
Cell["5. Where the analogy stops: reaction against diffusion", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"Ricci flow is nonlinear, and curvature obeys a reaction\:2013diffusion equation: for the Riemann tensor \:2202R/\:2202t = \:0394R + R\:00b2 + R^\:266f (Hamilton), and on a surface exactly ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "t"]]], " = ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:0394", "g"]]], " K + 2K\:00b2 (\:00a73). Its model is ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "t"]]], " = \:0394u + u\:00b2: diffusion spreads, the reaction concentrates, and positive data can blow up in finite time."}], "Text"],
Cell["Worked by hand. For constant data u\:2080 > 0 the reaction alone, u\:2032 = u\:00b2, gives u = u\:2080/(1 \:2212 u\:2080t), infinite at t = 1/u\:2080. The round unit sphere is this case: g = (1 \:2212 2t)g\:2080 has K = 1/(1 \:2212 2t), so R = 2K = 2/(1 \:2212 2t) solves R\:2032 = R\:00b2 with \:0394R = 0 and blows up at t = \:00bd, when the sphere vanishes.", "Text"],
Cell["{Simplify[D[u0/(1 - u0 \:03c4), \:03c4] - (u0/(1 - u0 \:03c4))^2], Simplify[D[2/(1 - 2 \:03c4), \:03c4] - (2/(1 - 2 \:03c4))^2]}", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{"The page\:2019s comparison: ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "t"]]], " = ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "xx"]]], " + c u\:00b2 on a circle of length 2\:03c0 from 0.6 + 0.4 cos x, with c = 1 (reaction on) and c = 0 (the heat equation)."}], "Text"],
Cell["rdSolve[c_, m_ : 0.6, a_ : 0.4] := Quiet@NDSolveValue[{D[v[\:03c4, x], \:03c4] == D[v[\:03c4, x], {x, 2}] + c v[\:03c4, x]^2, v[0, x] == m + a Cos[x], v[\:03c4, 0] == v[\:03c4, 2 \:03c0]}, v, {\:03c4, 0, 4}, {x, 0, 2 \:03c0},\n    Method -> {\"MethodOfLines\", \"SpatialDiscretization\" -> {\"TensorProductGrid\", \"MinPoints\" -> 200}}];\nmaxu[f_, \:03c4_] := Max[Table[f[\:03c4, x], {x, 0, 2 \:03c0, 2 \:03c0/200}]];\nrd1 = rdSolve[1]; rd0 = rdSolve[0]; tEnd = rd1[\"Domain\"][[1, 2]];\ntHit = Quiet[\:03c4 /. FindRoot[maxu[rd1, \:03c4] == 60, {\:03c4, 0.95 tEnd, 0.5, tEnd}]];\ncmpGrid[{cmpRow[\"u_t = u_xx + u\:00b2\", \"max u reaches 60 at t\", page[\"rd\", \"blowT\"], tHit], {\"\", \"solver stops (blow-up) near t\", \"\", tEnd, \"\"},\n  cmpRow[\"u_t = u_xx\", \"max u at t = 4\", page[\"rd\", \"heatEnd\"], maxu[rd0, 4]]}]", "Input"],
Cell["LogPlot[{maxu[rd1, \:03c4], maxu[rd0, \:03c4]}, {\:03c4, 0, Min[2.5, tEnd]}, PlotRange -> {0.3, 60}, PlotStyle -> {orange, blue},\n AxesLabel -> {\"t\", \"max u\"}, PlotLegends -> {\"u_t = u_xx + u\:00b2 (reaction wins)\", \"u_t = u_xx (diffusion only)\"}, ImageSize -> 420]", "Input"],
Cell["What to see: Wolfram\:2019s method of lines reaches 60 at t = 1.316 and stops at the blow-up near t = 1.333; the page\:2019s explicit steps lag slightly and pass 60 at t = 1.325 (both read t \:2248 1.32). Without the reaction both reach 0.6073 at t = 4.", "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Fig. 3. ", FontWeight -> "Bold", FontColor -> RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11]], "With the reaction term the maximum blows up in finite time; without it the solution settles to its mean (the page\:2019s reaction\:2013diffusion chart)."}], "Text"],
Cell["The animator: change the mean m and the ripple a of the initial data m + a cos x and see whether the reaction wins. For small positive data diffusion spreads the heat first, but on a closed circle the mean itself grows like the ODE u\:2032 = u\:00b2, so every positive start blows up eventually; the time is about 1/m for small ripples.", "Text"],
Cell["Manipulate[With[{f = rdSolve[1, mM, mA]}, With[{te = f[\"Domain\"][[1, 2]]},\n   LogPlot[maxu[f, \:03c4], {\:03c4, 0, Min[te, 6]}, PlotRange -> {0.05, 100}, PlotStyle -> orange, AxesLabel -> {\"t\", \"max u\"},\n    PlotLabel -> Row[{\"blow-up near t = \", NumberForm[te, 3], \"   (1/m = \", NumberForm[1/mM, 3], \")\"}], ImageSize -> 400]]],\n {{mM, 0.6, \"mean m\"}, 0.2, 1.5, 0.05, Appearance -> \"Labeled\"}, {{mA, 0.4, \"ripple a\"}, 0, 0.6, 0.05, Appearance -> \"Labeled\"}, SaveDefinitions -> True]", "Input"],
Cell["Why the torus flattens and the sphere vanishes. On the torus Gauss\:2013Bonnet forces \:222cK dA = 0, so positive and negative curvature balance and the reaction cannot win: the flow irons the metric flat (\:00a74). On the sphere the total curvature is 4\:03c0 > 0 and the reaction wins, but only uniformly: the sphere shrinks while becoming round. Hamilton (1988) and Chow (1991) proved that on any closed surface the normalised flow reaches constant curvature. In three dimensions necks pinch and singularities form; Perelman\:2019s classification of them, and surgery, completed Hamilton\:2019s programme for the Poincar\:00e9 conjecture.", "Text"],
Cell["References", "Section"],
Cell["G. Khan, An Illustrated Introduction to the Ricci Flow, arXiv:2201.04923 (2022), pp. 14\:201333: the heat equation, curvature, Ricci flow as a geometric heat flow, and its differences from the heat equation. The source of this notebook\:2019s outline.", "Text"],
Cell["R. S. Hamilton, Three-manifolds with positive Ricci curvature, J. Differential Geom. 17 (1982) 255\:2013306: introduces Ricci flow. (Not opened for this notebook.)", "Text"],
Cell["R. S. Hamilton, The Ricci flow on surfaces, Contemp. Math. 71 (1988) 237\:2013262: convergence on surfaces. (Not opened.)", "Text"],
Cell["B. Chow, The Ricci flow on the 2-sphere, J. Differential Geom. 33 (1991) 325\:2013334: the sphere without a positivity assumption. (Not opened.)", "Text"],
Cell["G. Perelman, The entropy formula for the Ricci flow and its geometric applications, arXiv:math/0211159 (2002): the monotone functional. (Not opened.)", "Text"],
Cell["P. Li and S.-T. Yau, On the parabolic kernel of the Schr\:00f6dinger operator, Acta Math. 156 (1986) 153\:2013201: the Li\:2013Yau estimate Khan cites for heat equations on spaces of non-negative Ricci curvature. (Not opened.)", "Text"],
Cell["C. Villani, Optimal Transport, Old and New, Springer (2009), ch. 14: Ricci curvature as the distortion of narrow geodesic cones. (Not opened.)", "Text"],
Cell["\:00a9 2026 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"]
},
WindowTitle -> "Ricci Flow and the Curvature\:2013Laplacian Relations",
StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"
]
